<BaseLayout title="About SelectVerdict" description="Learn why SelectVerdict exists and the editorial principles behind our independent software research."><Breadcrumbs items={[{ label: 'Home', href: '/' }, { label: 'About' }]} /><PageIntro eyebrow="About" title="Independent research for consequential software choices." description="SelectVerdict exists to make software selection more understandable, evidence-based, and transparent." /><article class="section narrow prose"><h2>Our purpose</h2><p>Software buyers face dense feature lists, shifting prices, and advice shaped by commercial incentives. We create structured research that makes the source of a fact, the age of the evidence, and the reasoning behind a recommendation visible.</p><h2>Our principles</h2><ul><li><strong>Accuracy before volume.</strong> An empty library is better than an unreliable one.</li><li><strong>Facts and judgment stay distinct.</strong> Vendor documentation can establish a feature; it cannot establish whether that feature is right for you.</li><li><strong>Commercial relationships are disclosed.</strong> Affiliate revenue may support the publication, but it does not purchase a verdict.</li><li><strong>Corrections are part of the work.</strong> Software changes, and our pages should show when information was checked.</li></ul><h2>What independence means here</h2><p>SelectVerdict controls its editorial process. We do not publish paid rankings, fake testimonials, or unsupported scores. When hands-on testing has not occurred, we do not imply that it has.</p></article></BaseLayout>
